.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt KILL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kill ,
.Nm tkill ,
.Nm tgkill
.Nd send a signal to a process, process group or thread
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.Ft int
.Fn kill "pid_t pid" "int sig"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn kill
posts signal
.Fa sig
to the processes selected by
.Fa pid .
Signals 1 through 64 are accepted.
Signal 0 checks existence and permission without posting a signal.
.Bl -tag -width "pid < -1"
.It Fa pid No > 0
Select the process with this process ID, or the process of the thread
with this thread ID.
.It Fa pid No = 0
Select the caller's process group.
.It Fa pid No < -1
Select the process group whose ID is
.No - Ns Fa pid .
.It Fa pid No = -1
Select every process the caller can see, except the caller's own
process and the init of the caller's PID namespace, as on Linux; the
signal is posted only to those the caller may signal.
An init sends
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGKILL
so at a shutdown; see
.Xr init 8 .
.El
.Pp
A signal to a process is pending for the process as a whole, and any of
its threads that does not block it takes it.
The kernel wakes the thread the ID names when that thread would take it
now, or else the first thread that would: one that does not block the
signal and has not begun to end, and, unless the signal is
.Dv SIGKILL ,
is not stopped and is running or has neither an unblocked signal of its
own nor signal work assigned to it by a previous send, as Linux's
.Fn complete_signal
chooses.
When no thread would, one thread is made runnable without new signal
work, as
.Xr sigaction 2
describes.
An ignored signal is dropped before it is queued, unless the target thread
blocks it, waits for it through a signal wait, or is traced and the signal
is not
.Dv SIGKILL .
A process whose threads have all ended remains a target until its parent
reaps it, as on Linux: the signal is discarded and the call succeeds.
A kernel thread takes no signal: a permitted call succeeds and discards
it, as Linux's kernel threads ignore every signal.
A group request, and a request with a
.Fa pid
of \-1, posts the signal once to each selected process the caller may
signal, through its leader, even when the leader has ended while other
threads run, as on Linux: the leader's credentials decide the
permission, its mask whether an ignored signal is kept, and any thread
that does not block the signal takes it.
A signal accepted during a member's fork is kept for its new child too;
a send to one process or one thread is not.
A group request succeeds if at least one member is permitted, returns
.Er EPERM
if members exist but none is permitted, and returns
.Er ESRCH
if no member exists.
A request with a
.Fa pid
of \-1 succeeds if it selects any process, permitted or not, a process
whose threads have all ended and a kernel thread included, posting to
none when none is permitted, and returns
.Er ESRCH
only when it selects none, as on Linux.
.Pp
A privileged caller may signal any target: one with effective user ID 0
in the first user namespace that has not given up its capabilities with
.Fn capset ,
as
.Xr capget 2
describes.
A process of another user namespace has no such privilege, where Linux
lets a process privileged in a user namespace signal the processes of
that namespace and of the ones nested in it; see
.Xr unshare 2 .
Otherwise, either the caller's real or effective user ID must match the
target's real or saved user ID.
For
.Dv SIGCONT
only, membership in the target's session also permits the call.
This session exception does not apply to signal 0.
.Ss Thread signal calls
.Nm tkill
and
.Nm tgkill
post the signal to one thread, which alone may take it; musl's and GNU
libc's
.Fn raise
and
.Fn pthread_kill
use them.
.Nm tkill
takes a positive thread ID and a signal.
.Nm tgkill
takes a positive thread group ID, which is a process ID, a positive
thread ID, and a signal; the thread must belong to that process.
Zero and negative IDs are errors in these calls, never group selectors.
Their permission and signal rules are those of
.Fn kill .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 37 for
.Nm kill ,
238 for
.Nm tkill ,
and 270 for
.Nm tgkill .
For
.Nm tkill ,
EBX is the thread ID and ECX the signal.
For
.Nm tgkill ,
EBX is the thread group ID, ECX the thread ID, and EDX the signal.
On x86-64 the numbers are 62, 200 and 234, with the same arguments in RDI,
RSI and RDX.
Musl declares neither function; a direct
.Fn syscall
is needed to issue either.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn kill
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return 0 on success or the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
places a child in its own process group, sends
.Dv SIGTERM
with
.Ql "kill(-cpid, SIGTERM)" ,
and checks that the child's wait status reports that signal.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The signal is outside 0 through 64, or a thread ID or thread group ID
given to
.Nm tkill
or
.Nm tgkill
is not positive.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the target, or may not signal any member of
the selected group.
A
.Fa pid
of \-1 never returns this error.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No selected process exists, the selected group has no members, the
thread has ended, or
.Nm tgkill
was given a thread that does not belong to the process.
.Fn kill
also returns this error for the most negative 32-bit
.Fa pid ,
and for
.Fa pid
0 when there is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capget 2 ,
.Xr getuid 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr unshare 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
